  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Sun Jul 12 2026\n\nOn Tuesday, a large upper level ridge will be centered across the \nNorthern Plains with an upper level low developing across Southwest \nTexas. This low will become the focus for additional heavy rain \nTuesday into Thursday, especially across our southwest counties. \nPWAT values will again remain high through this time frame, so \nlocally heavy rainfall will be the main concern. The exact location \nand eventual track of this low will ultimately determine where the \nheaviest rain ends up during this time frame. At this time, the \nhighest rain totals look to be across the Northern Edwards Plateau, \nwith rain totals decreasing as you head north and east. Still, heavy \nrain will be possible across the Concho Valley and Northwest Hill \nCountry as well. There is still some uncertainty on exact rain \namounts, but several inches of rain are possible, especially across \nthe northern Edwards Plateau. Expect medium to high (60-100%) across \nour southern counties Tuesday and Wednesday, with medium (30-60%) \nrain chances across the Big Country. The high PWAT values in \ncombination with slow moving storms will result in the potential \nfor flooding, especially across the northern Edwards Plateau. \n\nTemperatures through much of the upcoming week are forecast to \nremain below normal. Highs Tuesday through Thursday will be in the \n80s for most locations, which is well below seasonal normals. \nOvernight lows will be in the mid 60s to lower 70s. \n\nOn Friday, the upper level low is forecast to slowly drift west. \nAs this occurs, rain chances will remain confined to our western \ncounties with dry conditions across the eastern half of the area. \nHighs on Friday will continue to be below normal, in the upper 80s\nto near 90. A slow warm up is then expected as we head into next \nweekend.",
